+// The Doppler's settling (the receiver profile's rr_dyn_tau_s; none at 0).
+// Each epoch's Dopplers carry rr_dyn_sigma (0.5 m/s) per g of the IMU's
+// largest specific force past 1 g since the epoch before (jobChecks), which
+// ends with the thrust -- but the receiver's loops do not settle with it. On
+// the rig's rp5 runs the satellites whose loops held through the boost were
+// the low ones (5-13 deg up; the high ones relock, at rr_relock_sigma), and
+// their Dopplers in the first coast epochs were 1.9, 1.1 and 0.7 m/s rms off
+// at 1, 2 and 3 s after the thrust tailed off, each at a tracking sigma of
+// 0.1-0.35 m/s. A near-horizon Doppler is nearly all horizontal velocity,
+// which act's 10 deg launch prior lets the heading explain: two of them 1 m/s
+// off turned a right heading 5.4 deg (zt), three 9 deg (v10), for the rest of
+// the coast. With rr_dyn_tau_s the allowance is the larger of that peak and
+// the IMU's g past 1 faded with rr_dyn_tau_s since (g_mem_), so the first
+// coast epochs carry the boost's allowance as it decays.
